  "query_preview": "..Patchy fog was visible on satellite imagery earlier this\nmorning, primarily limited to the Cache Valley and near area \nreservoirs (namely Deer Creek and Strawberry). Expect little\nchange heading into tomorrow as a ridge of high pressure slowly\npasses overhead. A very weak, dry system will cross the area\nWednesday night, but is unlikely to produce any impacts. Instead,\nvalley inversions will likely strengthen, resulting in more valley\nhaze along the Wasatch Front and a warming trend in the mountains.\n\nLate in the week, a shallow ridge will build into the southwestern\nUS, resulting in largely zonal flow through the weekend and into\nearly next week. A couple of shortwave troughs will move through\nthe mean flow, the first drawing moisture into northern Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming late Friday into Saturday. Considerable\nuncertainty still remains in terms of QPF and snowfall, depending\non exact flow direction and the southern extent of moisture into\nUtah. For example, the NBM 25th percentile for QPF between Friday\nand Saturday evening suggests only up to 0.15\" in the northern\nmountains, whereas the 75th percentile suggests 0.50-1.00\" of\nliquid equivalent. Another potential shortwave trough could cross\nthe area late Sunday into Monday, but similar to the prior wave, \nplenty of uncertainty remains. Guidance does favor overall\ncyclonic flow next week across the western US, with more active\nweather looking more likely to return.",
